1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:19,400
How do you know your app works? Not 'it looked fine when I tried it' — how do you know,
every time, automatically? That's this chapter. And it doubles as proof that the
architecture from Module six was worth the effort.

2
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:25,400
Here is what you will be able to do by the end of this video.

3
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:43,400
Two kinds. Unit tests are fast because they never launch the app — they check logic
directly. UI tests are slower: they launch the real app and tap it like a person would. You
want lots of the first and a handful of the second.

4
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:53,800
Here's a unit test. Read the name and you can predict what it checks before reading the body
— movie search and rating sort across categories.

5
00:00:53,800 --> 00:01:05,000
It creates a view model. Sets the search text. Checks the filtered result. Flips the sort
order. Checks the order changed. That's the whole shape: arrange, act, assert.

6
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Now here's the point I want to land, and it's the reason Module six mattered.

7
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:23,400
There's no storyboard in this test. No screen. No UIKit at all. That's only possible because
the search and sort logic lives in the view model rather than the view controller.

8
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:38,600
If that logic had stayed in the screen — the way a first calculator is usually written —
this test could not exist. Testability isn't something you add later. It's a consequence of
how you structured the code.

9
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:43,000
It goes further. These are three fakes from the test file.

10
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:58,600
The real profile repository writes to disk; this one keeps profiles in an array. The real
password store uses the Keychain; this one uses a dictionary. The real review fetcher calls
a network API; this one returns fixed data.

11
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:10,200
Each satisfies the same protocol as the real thing. So tests hand these in instead — and not
one line of production code had to change to allow it.

12
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:25,000
Think about what testing login would mean without this. Writing real files to disk. Storing
real passwords. Making real network calls. Slow, unreliable, and dependent on an internet
connection. This is the concrete payoff of dependency injection.

13
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:35,800
And this is a UI test. The app is being driven automatically — tapping a category, opening a
film, checking the reviews screen, moving to seat selection.

14
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:53,400
One technique here worth stealing. This test asserts that a button is NOT present. Reviews
can only be written from a booking, so the write button must never appear while browsing.
Checking that something is absent is often more valuable than checking it's there.

15
00:02:53,400 --> 00:03:15,000
Two practical notes for UI tests. Use accessibility identifiers rather than matching on
visible text, so your tests don't break when copy changes — and your app becomes more
accessible as a side effect. And always wait for an element to exist rather than asserting

16
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:26,600
Something worth noticing about the whole suite: read the test names in order and you have
the specification for the feature. Ownership. Confirmation. Showtime passed. One review per
item.

17
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:36,200
No comments, no separate document. And unlike a document, a test name can't go stale —
because when it stops being true, it fails.

18
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:38,700
Let's recap the key points.

19
00:03:38,700 --> 00:03:41,200
Here is your practice exercise.

20
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:48,800
The final chapter is the one that decides whether any of this work survives being handed to
somebody else.
